  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Thursday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: Skies are partly to mostly sunny at this\ntime across northern and central Nevada. Winds are breezy to\nlocally windy as well. Look for the winds to subside this \nevening, though some higher elevation locations could see their \nwinds remain up or increase during the overnight. Lows will be in \nthe 40s and 50s.\n\nCurrent synoptic situation has three distinct low pressure systems\nacross the western part of North America. These systems will all\nplay a role in affecting the weather across the forecast area for\nthe short term. Upper low number one is situated across\nsouthwestern Canada. Upper low two is just west of California. And\nupper low three is the remnants of Hurricane Rachel. Models are\ndepicting upper low two and Rachel coalescing Saturday out in the\neastern Pacific with upper low one diving to the south. For\ntomorrow, look for increasing clouds during the day along with\nhigher relative humidity values. Winds will also be on the\nincrease as well. A wind advisory has been issued for much of\ncentral and eastern Nevada with gusts of 50 mph expected. Southern\nLander and Eureka counties are on the cusp of the strong winds as\nwell but were considered borderline. Afternoon highs will be in \nthe 70s.\n\nBy Saturday night into Sunday, major changes are in store for the\nforecast as cooler trough energy (upper low one) will be dropping\nin from the Pacific Northwest states and will join with upper\ntroughs two and three. Remnants from Rachel will drive \nprecipitable water values up to around 0.80 to 0.90 inches, which \nhas the potential to surpass the previously recorded daily maximum\nvalue of 0.74 inches for October 11. In addition, the Weather \nPrediction Center has placed a slight risk for excessive rainfall \nfor east-central Nevada. Limited surface based CAPE of 100 J/kg \nwill exist, along with marginal instability. Some isolated \nthunderstorms are possible Sunday morning and afternoon. All of \nthese ingredients are depicting increasing potential for moderate\nto locally heavy rain across central and northeast Nevada. As a \nresult, have issued a flash flood watch for Nye, White Pine and \nportions of Elko counties. Concern exists with regards to \nrecently burned areas and these will need to be watched. There may\nneed to be additional locations placed in the watch area based on\nfuture model runs. Overnight lows will be in the 50s. Winds will \nremain on the strong side so will be keeping the advisory going.\n\nSunday night into Monday, the positively tilted upper trough will\nremain over Washington and Oregon Sunday night into Monday with\nrain continuing over much of northern and central Nevada. Overall\nrain amounts are expected to be quite robust with amounts of a\nhalf inch to an inch or more in spots. Highs Monday will be in the\n60s with lows in the 40s.\n\nRemnant shower activity continues Tuesday with clearing conditions\nWednesday through Friday with gradually warming temperatures.\nHighs will start out in the 60s Tuesday with warming conditions\nin the 70s by Friday. Lows will be in the 30s and 40s.\n\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: Changes were made in the short\nterm through Sunday and Monday, increasing wind speeds and wind\ngusts, PoPs, and weather for the upcoming system. QPF has also\ncome up as well. High forecast confidence regarding strong wind \ngusts in Central Nevada continues for Saturday and Sunday. High \nforecast confidence regarding significant rainfall in Eastern \nNevada Sunday into Monday. High forecast confidence in a cooling \ntrend Saturday through Monday.",
